      ":question/id": "ce7d1760-2756-5375-938d-1131a0c95f14",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{- x y}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/10/10192762247eadd281a808157cc6d060c874ac1b23d4e48b98d6fdd7c48cb31d.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/aa/aa435546fcfb537b56b59dc6da3c426f86a9605f035f71bccaa25073988d411c.png)",
